---
// /how-it-works — the mechanism on one page: what a write does, what a
// read does, what lands in the bucket, where it does not help. Facts
// come from docs/product/explanation/delta-compression.md; keep the two
// in step. Plan: docs/plan/site-rewrite-2026-11.md §4.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import JsonLd from '../components/JsonLd.astro';
import WritePath from '../components/drawings/WritePath.astro';
import ReadPath from '../components/drawings/ReadPath.astro';
import { webPageSchema, breadcrumbListSchema } from '../lib/seo';

const title = 'How it works — DeltaGlider';
const description =
  'What DeltaGlider does on a PUT and on a GET: it stores a file as a binary diff against a reference when that saves space, and it checks the SHA-256 of every rebuilt file before it sends the first byte.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <Fragment slot="json-ld">
    <JsonLd schema={webPageSchema({ path: '/how-it-works', title, description })} />
    <JsonLd
      schema={breadcrumbListSchema([
        { name: 'DeltaGlider', path: '/' },
        { name: 'How it works', path: '/how-it-works' },
      ])}
    />
  </Fragment>

  <section class="hero">
    <div class="shell">
      <h1>How it works</h1>

      <p class="hero-lede">
        Versions of the same build, backup or release are almost the same
        file. DeltaGlider stores the first one whole and the others as
        the difference from it. Your clients send and receive whole
        files, as before.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="shell hiw">
    <section>
      <h2>What a write does</h2>

      <WritePath />

      <p>
        The first delta candidate that arrives in a folder becomes the
        reference of that folder. Every later file is compared with that
        reference, and never with another delta. So any version is
        rebuilt in one step, and a damaged delta cannot damage another
        version.
      </p>

      <p>
        The reference belongs to one folder, not to the bucket. Versions
        of one artifact are usually in one folder, and that is where the
        files are similar.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What a read does</h2>

      <ReadPath />

      <p>
        The client gets an ordinary S3 response, with the size, the ETag
        and the metadata of the file that it uploaded. When the hashes
        do not match (a damaged delta or a damaged reference), the proxy
        answers with an error. You can get an error, but you do not get
        wrong bytes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What is in your bucket</h2>

      <p>
        The same layout on S3 and on a local disk. You can decode any
        <code>.delta</code> file by hand with the stock
        <code>xdelta3</code> tool, without the proxy.
      </p>

      <pre><code>releases/
  firmware/widget-3000/
    reference.bin            the first upload, kept whole
    fw-1.4.1.tar.delta       about 60 KB
    fw-1.4.2.tar.delta
    release-notes.txt        not a delta candidate, stored as it is</code></pre>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Where it does not help</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Images, video and other compressed media.</strong> The
          proxy stores them whole and does not try.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong><code>.tar.gz</code> archives.</strong> Gzip compresses
          the whole stream as one unit, so a small change early in the
          archive changes every byte after it. A plain
          <code>.tar</code>, a <code>.zip</code> or a <code>.jar</code>
          of the same content deltas well.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Files with no earlier version.</strong> One file is one
          file.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        In each case the file is stored whole and costs what it costs on
        plain S3. <a href="/docs/explanation/delta-compression">The full
        explanation</a> covers the memory and disk use of large files.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What it costs</h2>

      <p>
        An upload that is stored as a delta is slower, because the proxy
        computes the diff before it answers: 9&nbsp;MB/s against
        106&nbsp;MB/s in our measurement. Downloads are about the same
        speed. <a href="/build-artifacts#speed">The table and the
        method</a>.
      </p>

      <div class="hero-ctas">
        <a href="/docs/tutorials/first-delta-savings" class="btn btn-primary">Follow the tutorial</a>
        <a href="/pricing#calculator" class="btn">Estimate your savings</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hiw section { margin: 4rem 0; }
  .hiw section > p { margin-top: 1.5rem; }
  .hiw pre { max-width: var(--measure-wide); margin-top: 1.25rem; }
  .hiw ul { max-width: var(--measure); }
  .hiw li { margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
</style>
